## Declaration of interest
Highlights
UiO-66 showed excellent structural stability against water, chloroform and dimethylformamide. Pristine UiO-66 and its solvent aged samples showed good selectivity for anionic dyes
against cationic dyes.
UiO-66 exhibited the highest adsorption selectivity for anionic dyes after aging in water for 12 months.
UiO-66 exhibited the maximum adsorption capacity for anionic methyl orange dye.
Abstract
The presence of dyes as pollutants in wastewater sources from textile industries can cause major health issues if they are not properly treated. Dye adsorption to entrap pollutants in nanoparticle pores has created a significant attention in recent years due to the environmental concerns occurring as a result of spillage of dyes in water bodies. In efforts to understand adsorption capability UiO-66 metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) were developed and examined for the separation of four pollutant dyes containing methyl red (MR), methyl orange (MO), malachite green (MG), and methylene blue (MB) that are widely used in textile industries. The adsorbent structural stability in water, chloroform, and dimethylformamide (DMF) were studied for one year period to understand their chemical stability. The UiO-66 crystalline structure remained unchanged in water and chloroform, while XRD patterns aged in DMF showed only a minor change, which might be due to the partially exterior framework collapse. The maximum adsorption capacities of pristine UiO-66 for anionic methyl red (MR) $(384\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ and methyl orange (MO) $(454\mathrm{mg/g)}$ dyes were considerably higher than those of cationic malachite green (MG) $(133\mathrm{mg/g)}$ and methylene blue (MB) $(370\mathrm{mg/g})$ dyes, particularly at lower pHs. The adsorption results perfectly matched the pseudo-second-order kinetic model. The MR, MO, and MB adsorption isotherms have matched with Langmuir model, while MG isotherms are in line with the Freundlich model. The distinctive features of UiO-66 framework make it potentially applicable to the treatment of wastewater.
Keywords: Metal-Organic Framework; UiO-66; Selective adsorption; Highly stable; Dye removal.
## 1. Introduction
Organic dyes are widely utilized in various manufacturing sectors like plastics, printing, textiles, papers, etc. The failure to remove dyes from such wastes may end up in serious damage to the aquatic environment. The organic being non-biodegradable, toxic, being stable against sunlight would act as oxidizing agents and these need to be removed from the contaminated waste sources before releasing into the water bodies [1-4]. Different techniques such as bio-degradation, advanced oxidation, photofenton, electron-fenton, ozonation, and adsorption have been utilized to eliminate such organic pollutants from the contaminated water, but majority of the common methods feature some shortcomings. For instance, the traditional bio-degradation is not efficient for highly chemical stable and low biodegradable dyes. Radiation and ozonation methods are highly costly and produce several toxic byproducts in the removal process. Among the different techniques, adsorption holds a significant promise for dye elimination owing to its superiorities such as high efficiency, simple design and operation, low cost, negligible production of toxic byproducts, and shorter process time [5,6].
Several reports on dye-removal application of various adsorbents such as activated carbon, clay materials, waste foundry sand, magnetic nanoparticles, graphene and graphene oxide (GO), carbon nanotubes (CNTs), hydrogels, polymers, zeolite, nanodiamond, agricultural waste, and metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been investigated [5-9]. The MOFs, as a unique family of porous nanomaterials, are comprised of organic ligands and the desired metal clusters. In view of their threedimensional spatial network, tunable shapes and pore size, large surface area, and great affinity for various organic dyes, MOFs have received much attention as a potential organic dye removal adsorbent[10-12]. Despitetheir superiority, MOFs have several drawbacks such as low thermal stability and low stability under natural air, water, and other chemical solvents, which prohibit their practical applications as the attractive adsorbents [10,13].
Studies on dye adsorption of various MOFs have tried to address the shortcomings such as low adsorption capacity, weak adsorption selectivity, and low stability inside the water [14,15]. Therefore, a few specific MOFs have shown promising performance for dye adsorption. Zhao et al. [16] introduced a new approach to synthesize various MOFs such as HKUST-1, MOF-5, MOF-505, NbO-type, and NOTT101. They observed that their water stability and morphological features can be modified by adjusting the volume ratio of water to dimethylformamide (DMF). The HKUST-1 showed the highest stability against water [16].
The applications of zirconium-based MOFs (UiO-66-X, where X can be H, COOH, NH2, SO3H, etc.,) have created considerable attention because of their high adsorption capacities and exceptional water stability [17-19].UiO-66- $N H_{2}$ nanoparticles possess higher adsorption capacity compared to UiO-66 for the cationic dyes due to electrostatic attraction arising from the high negative zeta potential [17]. In another study, water stability of isoreticularZr-MOFs constructed from different carboxylates was investigated [20], but no significant changes in the XRD pattern of UiO-66 were observed after aging in various environments due to inorganic unit strength, where each $Z r_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}$ unit is 12-coordinated to terephthalic acid ligands [20-22].In our previous study, it was observed that crystalline structure of UiO
66 did not change even after one year of aging in liquid water, but its adsorption affinity for anionic and cationic dyes showed slight drop after stirring in water at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for one year [18]. Therefore, in this study, UiO-66 was selected as an adsorbent because of its excellent water and thermal stability, high adsorption capacity toward organic dyes and good chemical resistance against polar and organic solvents like alcohols, aqueous HCl, acetone, aqueous NaOH, dimethylformamide, benzene, and chloroform [18,20,22,23].
In this study, UiO-66 was synthesized and its adsorption behaviors were investigated using adsorption kinetics, isotherm behaviors, and adsorption selectivity against different organic dyes, including MO, MG, MR, and MB. Then, the structural stability in various solvents like water, chloroform, and DMF after one-year aging was studied. To the best of our knowledge, such a systematic investigation was not available in the literature. The chemical structures of MR, MO, MG, and MB dyes are depicted in Scheme 1.
## Materials and Methods
## 1.1. Materials
Terephthalic acid, zirconium chloride $(Z r C l_{4})$ , DMF, chloroform, negatively charged MR andMOand positively charged MG and MBwere procured fromMerck.
## 1.2. UiO-66 Synthesis
For UiO-66 synthesis, $0.39{\tt g}$ of terephthalic acid and $0.54\:\mathrm{g}$ of $Z r C l_{4}$ were dissolved in $31~\mathsf{m L}$ DMF and agitated at $25^{\circ}C$ to obtain a homogeneous solution. The mixture was heated at $\boldsymbol{120^{\circ}C}$ in an autoclave and cooled to ambient temperature, which then centrifuged at 7000 rpm for 15 min to separate the nanoparticles. Subsequently, white nanoparticles were washed twice with DMF and then with
chloroform in five consecutive days. At the end, nanoparticles were dried at $\mathsf{150^{\circ}C}$ under vacuum overnight
## 1.3. Long-term aging experiments
Long-term structural stability of the nanoparticles as a challenging bottleneck in industrial applications was assessed in distilled water, DMF, and chloroform media. The suspension of UiO-66 nanoparticles within different solvents was kept at ambient temperaturefor two periods of 6 and 12 months. Centrifugation was performed to separate the aged nanoparticles. Finally, the aged UiO-66 was washed with fresh chloroform under sonication for 15 min and heated in a vacuum oven at $\boldsymbol{150^{\circ}C}$ .
## 1.4. Characterization
The crystalline structure of UiO-66 nanoparticles was evaluated using an X-pert Philips, pw 3040/60 Xray diffractometer (XRD). The morphology of pristine UiO-66 before and after aging was assessed by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM–MIRA3TESCAN). The surface area of the samples, degassed at $\boldsymbol{150^{\circ}C}$ under vacuum, was performed using ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms at $-196^{\circ}\mathsf{C b y}$ a BELSORP-miniII gas adsorption apparatus. Thermal stability was performed on a Perkin-Elmer Pyris thermogravimetric analyzer at temperature spectrum of 35 to $\boldsymbol{810^{\circ}C}$ with a heating ramp of $10^{\circ}C/\min$ .
## 1.5. Batch adsorption performance, kinetics and equilibrium studies
In order to investigate the UiO-66 adsorption mechanism, pseudo-first-order, pseudo-second order, and intra-particle diffusion modleswere used. The adsorbate uptake into the nanoparticles at time, t $(\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ $\left(\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{g}\right))$ and equilibrium $({\sf q_{e}}({\sf m g/g}))$ were measured using [24,25]:
$$
{\mathfrak{q}}_{\mathrm{t}}={\frac{({\mathrm{{C}}}_{0}-{\mathrm{{C}}}_{\mathrm{{t}}})\mathrm{{V}}}{\mathrm{m}}}
$$
$$
\mathsf{q}_{\mathsf{s}}=\frac{(\mathbf{C}_{0}-\bar{\mathbf{C}}_{\mathsf{s}})\mathrm{V}}{\mathrm{m}}
$$
where ${\mathsf C}_{0},{\mathsf C}_{\mathrm t},$ and ${\mathsf C}_{\mathrm{e}}({\mathsf m}\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{L})$ are dye concentrations at the start, any time t, and at equilibrium, respectively, adsorbent mass is m (g), and V (L) represents the dye solution volume.
The linear shapes of pseudo-first-order, pseudo-second-order, and intra-particle diffusion kinetic models are, respectively given as [26]:
$$
\ln(\ensuremath{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{s}}-\ensuremath{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{t}})=\ln(\ensuremath{\mathbf{q}}_{\mathrm{s}})-\ensuremath{\mathbf{K}}_{1}\ensuremath{\mathbf{t}}
$$
$$
\frac{\mathrm{~t~}}{\mathrm{q_{t}}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{K_{2}\mathrm{q_{\bar{s}}^{2}}}}+\frac{\mathrm{~t~}}{\mathrm{q_{\bar{s}}}}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathbb{q}_{\mathtt{t}}=\mathbb{K}_{\mathtt{i d}}\mathbb{t}^{1/2}+\mathbb{C}}\end{array}
$$
Here, $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ and ${\sf q_{e}}\left({\sf m g/g}\right)$ arethe amountofadsorbed dye at any time, t and equilibrium,respectively; $\mathsf{K}_{1}$ $(\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}),\mathsf{K}_{2}(\mathsf{g}/\mathsf{m g}.\mathsf{m i n})$ and $\mathsf{K}_{\mathrm{id}}(\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{g}\mathsf{m i n}^{1/2})$ are the rate constants of pseudo-first-order, pseudo-secondorder and intra-particle diffusion models, respectively; C is related to the boundary layer thickness (constant).
Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm models were employed to determine the adsorption mechanism as well as theoretical maximum adsorption capacity. According to Langmuir model, the adsorbate homogeneously covers the adsorbent surface and makes a uniform monolayer. Freundlich adsorption model assumes the heterogeneous-multilayer presence of the adsorbate onto the adsorbents surface, while adsorption capacity is dependent on the equilibrium. The linear forms of the modelsare given below [25]:
$$
\frac{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{e}}}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{e}}}}=\frac{1}{\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{L}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{max}}}}+\frac{\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{e}}}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{max}}}}
$$
$$
\ln{\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}=\ln{\bf K}_{\mathrm{F}}+\frac{1}{{\bf n}_{\mathrm{F}}}\ln{\bf C}_{\mathrm{e}}\left(7\right)
$$
Here, $\mathtt{q}_{\mathtt{m a x}}$ is theoretical maximum adsorption capacity $\left({\sf m g/g}\right)$ when the dye molecules create a uniform monolayer on adsorbents surface, while $\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{L}}(\mathsf{L}/\mathsf{m}\mathsf{g})$ is Langmuir constant according to the binding site affinity, which defines the adsorption energy. The $K_{F}$ and ${\mathfrak{n}}_{\mathtt{F}}$ are Freundlich constants, connected to the capacity and strength of adsorption, respectively. The value of $1/\mathsf{n}$ determines the adsorption mechanism tendency and the extent of adsorbent surface heterogeneity [27].
A dimensionless constant can be defined to examine the adsorption favorability of UiO-66 as shown in Eq. 8 [8,28]:
$$
\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}=\frac{1}{1+\mathsf{C}_{0}\mathsf{K}_{\mathrm{L}}}
$$
Here, C0is thedye concentration, and $k_{\mathrm{L}}$ isLangmuir constant. The favorable adsorption isotherm usually happens for the RLvalues in the range of 0 to 1.
## 1.6. Selective separation
A solution of 5 mg UiO-66 nanoparticle and $25~\mathsf{m L}$ dye mixture with a concentration of $20\mathrm{mg/L}$ ( $\mathrm{10~mg/L}$ for each dye) was mixed to assess the selectivity and separation behavior of the nanoparticle. After three-hour adsorption at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}_{\iota}$ , the adsorbent was separated and UV-Vis spectroscopy was performed to determine the dye concentration.
The ideal adsorption selectivity (S) was determined from the single adsorption isotherms using [26]:
$$
\mathrm{S}{=}\frac{\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{i}}}{\mathrm{q}_{\mathrm{j}}}
$$
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Adsorbents characterization
XRD images of pristine and solvent aged UiO-66 are displayed in Figure 1. XRD tracings before and after aging provide an insight into the crystalline structure stability of the nanoparticles. As shown in Figures 1a and 1b, the profiles of pristine UiO-66 and aged samples in water and chloroform are quite identical and show similar pattern and are agreeing with the literature [20]. The appearance of three distinctive peaks at $2\theta=7.38$ , 8.56, and $25.67^{\circ}$ indicate that UiO-66 was successfully synthesized, this did not have any impurity in its crystalline structure.
Moreover, XRD patterns show no crystalline structure failure upon aging in water and chloroform for 12 months, but structural changes in the XRD pattern of UiO-66 aged in DMF (D-UiO-66) are visible. Figure 1c demonstrates that observed peaks intensities at $2\theta=7.38$ and $8.56^{\circ}$ decreased and a new peak appearedat $2\theta=40.02^{\circ}$ . Besides, XRD profile of UiO-66 aged in DMF presents a broad peak at $2\theta=6.5–10^{\circ}$ , indicating that the crystalline structure of UiO-66 has collapsed in the presence of DMF and deformed to an amorphous structure [29]. The emergence of a new peak at $2\uptheta=40.02^{\circ}$ in the XRD profiles of DMF aged samples might be due to the recrystallization of terephthalate linker within the pores of UiO-66 as a result of solvation reaction [22,30,31]. The new peak intensity was enhanced by increasing the aging time. Overall, XRD revealed that MOF framework did not entirely collapse even after long-term aging in DMF.
FESEM micrographs of UiO-66 before and after aging in various environments are depicted in Figure 2. The non-agglomerated pristine UiO-66 has a cubic shape with a crystal size of about $100\mathsf{n m}$ . The morphological observation shows that surface of pristine UiO-66 nanoparticles is much rougher than that of the solvent aged samples (particularly in water), suggesting that UiO-66 crystals are deformed on the exterior surface.
The nanoparticle\`s dimension might have grown in size after aging in water and chloroform to formlarge agglomerates. However, DMF aged samples not only displayed very little evidence of agglomeration over the aging samples of for 12 months, but also they were slightly shrunkdue to the collapse of the exterior framework as endorsed by XRD analysis.
Structural stability assessment was done by nitrogen adsorption analysis, where ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms and pore features of pristine and aged UiO-66 are presented in Figure 3. The corresponding data calculated from the BET analysis, including BET surface area, pore-volume, and average pore size are also given in Table 1. As per Figure 3, it is obvious that pristine UiO-66 nanoparticles showed type-I isotherm, illustrating the microporous structure [20], and also displayed a uniform and narrow pore size distribution at $1.7\mathsf{n m}$ .
Figure 3. The ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms (desorption in red color) and pore size distribution of pristineUiO-66 and aged samples in (a) water, (b) chloroform, and (c) DMF at different times. The aging of nanoparticle in various solvents significantly decreased the BET surface area in the first six months, followed by a slight reduction during the second six months (Table 1). The surface area was reduced due to aggravation by enhancing the polarity of the solvent. This observation corroborates XRD and FESEM data, which showed the failure in crystalline structures anddeformations in the outer surface morphology because of exposure to the harsh atmosphere. According to Table 1, the UiO-66 sample aged in DMF for 12 months (D-UiO-66-12) experienced the highest reduction in BET surface area (more than $30\%$ ), probably due to the framework collapse and partially pore blockage originated from the recrystallization of free terephthalate linker and DMF molecules within the pores. Meanwhile, the sample aged in chloroform displayed a negligible BET surface area loss $(14\%)$ after 12 months, reflecting the retention of porous structure, which is in line with FESEM and XRD results.
The pore volume and average pore diameter of the adsorbent enhanced with aging in different solvents (Table 1), which supports the pore size distribution results from Figure 3. All the aged samples possess pore diameter $>2\mathsf{n m}$ (Table 1 and Figure 3), indicating the mesoporous structures of the aged samples. The long-time exposure of the nanoparticle to different solvents changed the microporous structure of the nanoparticles to mesoporous.
TGA thermograph of pristine UiO-66 shows three distinct weight reduction steps in the temperature range of 45-220, 280-370, and $470–600^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ that are centered at 95, 320, and $550^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , respectively (Figure 4a). The weight reduction at $95^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ might be due to evaporation of water and solvents captured in the pores or adsorbed onto the nanoparticle surface. The weight reduction for W-UiO-66 is higher than the other solvent aged samples, unveiling greater adsorption of water molecules [22]. The weight reduction centered at $320^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ represents the loss of unreacted organic ligand and trapped solvent in the pores of UiO-66, which happened at a higher temperature than their boiling point due to theirstronginteractions with the pore walls. The final weight reduction at $550^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ is associated with the collapse and degradation of the framework into $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , CO, and zirconium oxide $(Z r O_{2})$ [18,32,33].
As can be seen from Figure 4b, the samples aged in DMF and chloroform displayed small shifts in the inflection points. Accordingly, in case of D-UiO-66-12, the first and second weight reductions decreased to 70 and $270^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , respectively, while the final weight reduction slightly increased to $555^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ and a new small weight reduction occurred at $440^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which might be due to the presence of terephthalate crystals within the pores of UiO-66. TGA thermograms of D-UiO-66-12 and UiO-66 are nearly alike, implying that framework of the aged sample was not completely damaged and its thermal stability is still high.
## 2.2. Adsorption kinetics
The influence of adsorbent/adsorbate exposure time on adsorption capacity, as a key factor in practical applications, was assessed, and the results are depicted in Figure 5. The adsorption capacity of pristine UiO-66 increased rapidly in a short period of time $\ensuremath{\left[15\mathrm{\min}\right]}$ ), which then experienced a very gentle increment to reach a relatively unchanged value with further contact time. The adsorption was then stopped after approximately two hours (equilibrium condition) [27]. The MR and MO adsorption capacities of UiO-66 are markedly greater than those of cationic MB and MG dyes (Figure 5a). It is apparent from Figures 5b and 5d that adsorption capacity of pristine UiO-66 decreased by aging in different solvents, and the adsorption capacity declined with increasing aging time and solvent polarity. In consequence, samples aged in water and chloroform showed the lowest and highest adsorption capacity, respectively.
The adsorption kinetic models were employed to ascertain the rate-limiting step during the kinetics of dyes adsorption. The results obtained are compiled in Table 2. The correlation coefficients (R2) for pseudo-second-order kinetic model are slightly greater than the other models, but are very close to one. Furthermore, fitting of the adsorption capacity values $(\mathsf{q}_{\mathsf{e},\mathsf{c a l}})$ from the model with the experimental adsorption capacity confirms the validity of pseudo-second-order kinetic model. This observation sugegstesthat chemisorption is the rate-limiting step in dye adsorption [34].
## 2.3. Adsorption isotherms
Adsorption isotherms at various initial dye concentrations were performed to assess equilibrium data and adsorbate/adsorbent interactions to find the adsorption mechanism. The UiO-66 adsorption capacity at various initial dye concentrations is presented in Figure 6. As can be observed, ${\mathsf{q}}_{\mathrm{e}}$ parameter of dyes enhanced with an initial dye concentration increment and reached maximum values of 329, 401, 82, and $326~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for MR, MO, MG and MB dyes, respectively for the initial dye concentration equal of $200~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ . The adsorption results matched well with the Langmuir and Freundlich modelsto evaluate adsorption mechanism.
A comparison of our results with the literature findings (summarized in Table 4) suggests that pristine UiO-66 displays the best adsorption performance for anionic MR and MO dyes adsorption from aqueous solutions as $\mathsf{q}_{\mathsf{m a x}}1$ for MO, MB, and MR dyes are considerably greater than those listed, while adsorption capacity for cationic MG dye is slightly lower than other adsorbents. Thus, $\mathsf{q}_{\mathsf{m a x}}$ of pristine UiO-66 for different organic dyes mainly depends on the size and charge of the dyes.
## 2.4. Effect of initial pH and ionic strength on adsorption capacity
The interactions of adsorbents with dye molecules depend on the initial pH of dye solutions due the influence of pH on the adsorbent surface charge as well as dye molecules dependence on the pH. Thus, the effect of intialsolution pH on adsorption capacities $(\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}})$ of these dyes at $25^{\circ}($ and at different pHs (from 4 to 12) was assessed and the results are shown in Figure 8a. The $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\mathsf{o}^{\mathsf{f}}$ all the dyes display different reactions towards the initial pH value. The $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\mathsf{o}^{\mathsf{f}}$ anionic MR and MO dyes decreased gradually by increasing the pH and showed the lowest values at $\mathsf{p H}{\approx}12$ . A plausible explanation for this observation might be the electrostatic repulsion increment among the anionic dyes and the highly negative charge of the pristine UiO-66. The $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\mathsf{v}$ alues of UiO-66 for MR and MO dyes at $\mathsf{p H}{\approx}\mathsf{1}2$ are high as show in Figure 8a. This suggests the role of other adsorption mechanisms such as π-π stacking interactions between the aromatic structure of dye molecules and benzene ring of UiO-66, hydrogen bonding interactinsbetween the polar functional groups of dye molecules and the surface of UiO-66 nanoparticles in addition to physical adsorption and penetration of dye molecules into the porous structure of pristine UiO-66 [18].
The $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\mathsf{o}^{\mathsf{f}}$ both cationic MG and MB dyes onto this adsorbent increased rapidly with an increasing pH, which reached the maximum, then decreased by increasing the solution pH.Such an unusual trend could be justified by the varying interactions between the cationic dyes and the surface of the adsorbent nanoparticles caused by the zeta potential variation with pH.Figure 8c shows that surface charge of pristine UiO-66 reduces gradually with increase of pH, suggesting the presence of highly negative surface charge. However, in acidic pH, electrostatic repulsion forces between the cationic dyes and the positively charged surface of UiO-66 is predominantly due to adsorption mechanism, confirming the lowest adsorption capacity. By increasing the pH of dye solutions, repulsive forces decrease and attractive force becomes dominant between the cationic dye molecules and the negatively charged surface of UiO-66, contributing to an increase in ${\mathsf{q}}_{\mathrm{e}}$ of the cationic dyes with enhancedpH.
The impact of salt concentration (ionic strength) on dye adsorption capacity of pristine UiO-66 was also examined in different ionic strength solutions adjusted by NaCl. It can be seen from Figure 8b that the trends of variation of ${\mathsf{q}}_{\mathrm{e}}$ of anionic and cationic dyes in pristine UiO-66 are the same, and the adsorption capacities of dyes decreased by increasing the ionic strength of dye solutions. This might be due to the screening influence of chlorine ions (Cl-), which would reduce electrostatic attraction between the dye molecules and the surface of adsorbents [7].
## 2.5. Selective separation
Selective adsorption ability of UiO-66 against different dyes in the binary solution containing various dyes with different charges was also investigated and these data are displayed in Figure 9. The UV-Vis adsorption spectra show that adsorption peak intensities of anionic MR and MO dyes decrease noticeably after $2h$ , while those of the cationic MG and MB dyes decreased slightly. This suggests that pristine UiO-66 could selectively separate the anionic dyes from anionic/cationic dyes mixture, which leads to color switch of the mixture from dark to blue.
The ideal adsorption selectivity of MR and MO against MG and MB onto pristine and solvent aged adsorbents was calculated based on their single adsorption experiments. Figure 10 describes that ideal adsorption selectivities of MR/MG and MO/MG are significantly higher than those of MR/MB and MO/MB. This might be because of the smaller size of MB than MG, which may penetrate through the small pores of the adsorbents. The highest adsorption selectivity was obtained by W-UiO-66-12, which is attributed to increase of zeta potential caused by the hydrolysis reaction, which could change interaction between the dye molecules and adsorbents. As a consequence of hydrolysis, the bond between inorganic unit and organic ligands is broken, so several carboxylic acid functionalities are created in the outer surface of the aged UiO-66, leading to increase of zeta potential of W-UiO-66-12. Additionally, this reaction can create much defect sites on the surface of water aged UiO-66, which might have increased the adsorption capacity for anionic dyes [31,48,49]. In e case of W-UiO-66-12, the electrostatic forces between anionic dyes and the highly positive charge of the adsorbent increased, while electrostatic repulsion between cationic dyes and the adsorbent increased, thereby giving an increase in $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ of the anionic dyes with a reduction in the $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\mathsf{v}$ alues of cationic dyes.
## 3. Conclusions
UiO-66, which is a chemical stable MOF, was successfully synthesized and employed for anionic and cationic dyes removal from the aqueous solutions. The pristine UiO-66 and its solvent aged samples were characterized by XRD, FE-SEM, BET, TGA, and zeta potential. Pristine UiO-66 displayed good structural stability against water, chloroform, and DMF based on XRD and TGA data with a negligible reduction of BET surface area, due to excessive structural strength of the inorganic units. While BET surface area of UiO-66 aged in DMF for 12 months showed $30\%$ reduction compared to pristine UiO-66, it still has the reasonable value as its adsorption capacity for organic dyes was quite desirable. The effect of contact time, initial pH, ionic strength, aging time, and initial dye concentration were examined and adsorption kinetics was adequately described by pseudo-second-order kinetic model, while equilibrium adsorption results were designated by the Langmuir isotherm model. The $\mathtt{q}_{\mathtt{m a x}}$ of pristine UiO-66 for MR, MO, MG, and MB dyes are 384, 454, 133, and $370~\mathrm{mg/g},$ respectively. Adsorption mechanism studies suggest that adsorption of anionic and cationic dyes onto UiO-66 can be explained as due to electrostatic interaction, hydrogen bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction; physical adsorption and dye molecules may be captured in the porous structure of UiO-66. The UiO-66 nanoparticles aged in water showed a greater tendency to adsorb anionic MO and MR dyes than the cationic MG, mainly due to powerful electrostatic attractions between the unlike charges of anionic dyes and W-UiO-66-12. In conclusion, from the present findings, UiO-66 nanoparticles are the potential candidates for selective dye pollutant removal from the wastewater sources.
